Polymorphism
means "many forms." It allows a single method or object to behave differently based on the context.

Two Types of polymorphism
1] run time polymorphism
2] compile time polymorphism

1] run time polymorphism :-
   Method Overriding :-  There is method with same method name with same parametrs in base class and derived class
   then we can call as method overloading.

   * co-varient return types allowed in method overriding.
     Example :- Int ---> Number
   * complelity different return types not allowed
     Example Int----> String

   Rules For Method Overriding

   - Same Method Signature ✅
     The method name and parameters must be exactly the same in both the base and derived classes.

   - Return Type: Same or Covariant ✅
     The return type must be the same or a subclass (covariant return type).

   - Access Modifier: Cannot Be More Restrictive ❌
     The overriding method cannot reduce visibility.
     Allowed: protected → public ✅
     Not Allowed: public → private ❌

   - Private, Static, and Final Methods Cannot Be Overridden

     private:- methods belong to the class, not the instance.
     static :- methods are not overridden but hidden.
     final  :- methods cannot be changed.


 2] Method Overloading [ Compile Time polymorphism ]

Two methods present in same class with same name and Different parameters.

*  Rules for Method Overloading

  - Methods Must Have the Same Name ✅
    The method name must be identical.

  - Different Number, DataType, or Order of Parameters ✅
    Overloaded methods must differ in at least one of these:
    Number of parameters
    Data type of parameters
    Order of parameters (if types are different)

  - Return Type Does Not Matter ❌
    Overloading is based only on parameters, not the return type.

* Static methods can be overloaded.








Q] Why static method cannot override ?
Ans :- In Java, static methods belong to the class rather than instances (objects).
       Since method overriding is based on runtime polymorphism (dynamic binding),
       static methods cannot be overridden because they are resolved at compile time (static binding).

Q] Why Static methods we are able to overload ?
Ans :-  Static methods can be overloaded because method overloading is based on compile-time (static) polymorphism.
        The compiler determines which method to call based on method signatures (number, type, or order of parameters),
        regardless of whether the methods are static or non-static.